{:db/id 17592186175912 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186175740 :question/id #uuid "1e0c95a6-24fc-55d1-9c4c-bf87877b3723" :question/math-academy-id "e-14729" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ and is parallel to the line passing through the points $(2,1)$ and $(6,4)$. Write your answer in **standard form**." :question/worked-solution "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(2,1)$ and $(6,4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 1}{6 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(1,-1)$. To find the equation of this line, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-1) &= \\frac{3}{4}(x - 1) \\\\\ny + 1 &= \\frac{3}{4}x - \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3}{4}x - \\frac{7}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write this equation in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{3}{4}x - \\frac{7}{4} \\\\\n4y &= 3x - 7 \\\\\n- 3x + 4y &= -7 \\\\\n3x - 4y &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a27c6f2-b81b-5b62-bcd0-14936842fe83" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049201 :topic/math-academy-id 3725 :topic/title "Properties of Lines Given in Standard Form"}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id 17592186482708 :question-generator/id #uuid "f306f06a-785e-5d4c-bd17-02ca8cc6dc42" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186175912.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186176419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "e84db149-0fdf-57ec-bf76-ee6bccc2080a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186174742} {:answer-field/id #uuid "053e9052-c4d0-592f-ae8e-2395eb179ccb" 